When people talk about AI models, terms such as &ldquo;reasoning&rdquo; and &ldquo;world model&rdquo; often appear next to &ldquo;chatbot&rdquo;, and in many cases it is not clear how they relate. In this post I explain four of these ideas through one everyday example, a newcomer who learns Kavala only from millions of taxi-trip descriptions, without ever seeing a map, and I show where attacks can enter at each step.
1. Predicting the next word # Seen through this example, the starting point is simple: given the text so far, the model guesses the most likely next word, then the next. After &ldquo;from the port, turn left onto&rdquo;, the newcomer can guess which street name usually follows. Today&rsquo;s large language models, including the most advanced, still work this way.
2. Reasoning models # Building on this base, reasoning models add two things. They write out intermediate steps before answering, and they are further trained on problems whose answers can be checked automatically, such as mathematics and code, so that steps leading to correct answers are reinforced.1 In our example, the newcomer now lists the turns one by one and checks the arrival time before announcing the route.
3a. A world model inside the model # An interesting finding came from a model trained only on lists of moves from the board game Othello. The model had formed its own internal picture of the board, and when the researchers changed a square in that picture, the model&rsquo;s next move changed to match.2 In our example, the newcomer turns out to carry a rough mental map of the city, and editing that map changes the suggested route. How complete such maps are in large models remains open.
3b. World models as a separate kind of model # The same term also names a different family of models, trained on video and interaction instead of text, whose task is to predict how an environment changes after an action.3 Yann LeCun argues that machines need such models to learn by observation and to plan, in a position paper that proposes a path rather than reporting a tested result.4 In our example, this is a driving simulator, trained on street footage, that shows the view after turning left at the port.
Where the attacks enter # Staying with the city, each added capability changes where an attacker can get in, forming four layers of one attack surface.
Prediction. Misleading text placed in the input can steer what the model writes next, known as prompt injection (OWASP LLM01:2025, ATLAS AML.T0051),56 in our example, a fake street sign in a trip description. Reasoning. The written steps are not always the steps the model actually used, a property called unfaithful reasoning,7 and models trained to keep those steps clean can learn to hide unwanted behaviour from the text while still doing it.8 Thus a route can look tidy on paper while the car follows another one. Internal map. Anyone with access to the model&rsquo;s internal values can add or remove directions in them (activation steering, close to ATLAS AML.T0018), and removing a single direction was shown to stop many open models from refusing harmful requests.910 This resembles redrawing the newcomer&rsquo;s map so that a closed road appears open. Simulator. Inputs crafted to mislead the model while it runs (evasion, ATLAS AML.T0015)11 and corrupted training data (poisoning, ATLAS AML.T0020 and OWASP LLM04:2025)1213 can both distort its predictions, and in a robot a wrong prediction can become a wrong action, as with a simulator trained on footage where the closed road was always open. The other lens: these labels describe what was added to a model or found inside it, so in many cases one deployed system carries all four at once, and their points of entry overlap within it.
Overall, prediction remains the base, reasoning adds steps that can be checked, and world models, whether found inside language models or built as separate simulators, describe how well a model represents the place it talks about. The cost of each addition is a new place where the model can be misled or misread. As a next step, I would explain how researchers look inside these models, since that is how the internal map in 3a was found.
