When people hear that an AI assistant &ldquo;uses an index&rdquo;, they often imagine a component that makes the model itself smarter. The model does not change. The index decides which pieces of information reach it, and that decision is also one of the places where attacks begin.
I find a book with a good index at the back a useful everyday example. The reader is the language model, which reads and summarises well but can hold only a limited number of pages at once. A search step consults the index before reading starts and hands over the pages most likely to answer the question, a pattern known as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).1
Why the index helps # Seen this way, the index gives an assistant three properties that the model alone does not have:
relevance, since only the most useful pages fill the model&rsquo;s limited context window; current and private knowledge, since new or internal documents can be indexed without retraining the model; traceability, since a human can check which pages the answer came from. Vectors and embeddings # A classic book index lists words, and a keyword index does the same, matching the exact terms of the question. Many AI assistants add a meaning-based index. An embedding model turns each passage into a vector, a long list of numbers, so that passages with similar meaning lie close to each other. The question is turned into a vector in the same way, and the vector database returns its nearest neighbours, even when they share no words with the question. In the book, it acts less like the back index and more like a librarian who finds pages on the concept, whatever the wording. In many cases both are combined, since keywords find exact names and vectors find paraphrases.
Where the attacks enter # Staying with the book, whoever can slip a page into it, or read the index itself, can influence the answer. Direct prompt injection and jailbreaks arrive in the question itself; this section covers the attacks that come through the index. OWASP lists this family as LLM08:2025, Vector and Embedding Weaknesses,2 and MITRE ATLAS catalogues the poisoning case as technique AML.T0070, RAG Poisoning.3 Four of these risks follow directly from the mechanism above:
poisoning, a forged page placed where the index will point: in the PoisonedRAG study, injecting five crafted texts per target question into a knowledge base of millions of texts reached a 90% attack success rate;4 indirect prompt injection, a page that addresses the reader instead of informing it: a retrieved page can carry instructions that the model may follow as if they came from the user;5 access-control gaps, an index that points into a locked chapter: an index built from documents with different permissions may hand a page to a user who could not open the original;2 embedding inversion, an index detailed enough to rebuild the page: stored vectors can leak the text they encode, and the Vec2Text method recovered 92% of 32-token inputs exactly from their embeddings.6 These four need different access. Poisoning and indirect prompt injection need only a way to get content into the indexed sources, access-control gaps need only a misconfiguration, and embedding inversion needs read access to the stored vectors. The four steps in the figure, embed, index, retrieve and answer, cover how pages reach the model rather than every control; checks on what the model writes, such as OWASP&rsquo;s LLM05:2025 Improper Output Handling, come after the answer step.7
The other lens: at a systemic level, the index is often treated as plumbing, while the documents it points to are classified and access-controlled. In practice it is a second copy of that data, so it needs the source&rsquo;s classification, permissions and change review, with permissions checked at query time inside the vector store and retrieved text treated as data, not instructions.
Overall, an index lets a general model answer from specific, current and checkable pages. The cost is building it, keeping it current and securing it as a data store in its own right. As a next step, I would measure how keyword, vector and hybrid indexes differ in their exposure to poisoning, since each one ranks a forged page differently.
